  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 220 AM EDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nMoving into the upcoming weekend, Isaias will make landfall along \nthe northern Gulf Coast on Friday night and then move into the Deep \nSouth heading into Saturday. At the same time, mid level ridging in \nthe western Atlantic will strengthen as it gradually pushes over the \nFlorida Peninsula and ultimately into the eastern Gulf as the \nweekend progresses. The pressure gradient over the region will \nstill remain tight at the surface during the first part of the \nweekend due to Isaias to the northwest, and a strengthening large \narea of high pressure in the Atlantic. This will keep a breezy and\ngusty southeasterly wind flow in place at the surface and a more \nsoutherly wind flow aloft. This type of set up will continue to \nadvect deep tropical moisture into the region from the Carribean \nas the latest global and ensemble guidance shows PWAT values \nrising and ranging between 2.2 and 2.4 inches across the area on \nSaturday. This will keep the wet and unsettled pattern in place \nduring this time frame with higher chances of showers and \nthunderstorms across most of the region. While storm motion will \nbe much faster than it has been over the past several days, the \ndeep tropical moisture will continue to allow for the possibility \nof very efficient rainfall rates with the stronger storms. This \nwill keep the possibility of localized flooding in place across \nthe urban and poor drainage areas as well as locations that are \nalready saturated from recent heavy rainfall. \n\nDuring the second half of the weekend the latest global and ensemble \nguidance suite continues to suggest that mid level ridging will \ncenter over the Florida Peninsula on Sunday before shifting westward \ninto the Gulf on Monday while quickly flattening over South \nFlorida. This flattening and westward shift will be caused by the \nremnants of Isaias pushing across the Mid-Atlantic States and \ninto the western Atlantic well off to the north on Monday. At the \nsurface, high pressure will build over the region on Sunday before\nstarting to shift into the Gulf on Monday. This will allow for \nthe pressure gradient to decrease across the area and \nsoutheasterly winds will gradually decrease during this time \nframe. At the same time, deeper layer subsidence will bring some \ndrier air back into the region which will bring a typical late wet\nseason type pattern back into the region for Sunday and maybe \neven lower chances of showers and storms heading into Monday. High\ntemperatures on Sunday and Monday will generally range from \naround 90 along the east coast to the lower 90s across interior \nportions of Southwest Florida. \n\nHeading into Tuesday and Wednesday, the forecast uncertainty does \nincrease a little bit as it is towards the end of the forecast \nperiod, but the latest global and ensemble guidance is hinting at \nthe potential for a weakening front to approach and push through \nthe area sometime towards the middle of the week. This would \nsignificantly decrease rain chances behind the front as the \nbeginning of the dry season approaches. This will continue to be \nmonitored as the week progresses.",
